WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.860
Supermarkets.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:15.480
It’s hard to avoid these modern temples
of convenience and consumerism.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:20.579
And like the fictitious temples from adventure
stories, they’re filled with pitfalls and

00:00:20.579 --> 00:00:24.769
booby traps designed to make you spend more
than you planned.

00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:28.820
As any good treasure-hunter will tell you,
you can never let your guard down.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.670
Satipo: “There is nothing to fear here!’

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:34.410
Indy: “That’s what scares me.”

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:39.750
But with a little preparation and discipline,
you can safely swing past every metaphorical

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:44.180
snakepit, rolling boulder and... alien spaceship?

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.020
Let’s pretend that one didn’t happen.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.100
Okay.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:55.640
[MUSIC]

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.000
From the moment you walk through the front
doors, your senses are getting played.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.710
Bright, colorful flowers, the smell of fresh-baked
bread, soft, comfortable music.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:10.680
All these things are designed to put you in
a relaxed, suggestive state of mind so that

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.530
you’ll take your time and be more open-minded
about what you came to buy.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:19.290
If you’re like most people, you probably
start in the produce section, and that’s

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:20.570
no accident.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:24.560
Stores have found that when people stock their
cart with healthy things early on, they’re

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.030
more likely to reward themselves with junk
food later.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:33.060
It may be tempting to grab some of those pre-chopped
fruits and vegetables, but take a second to

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.860
compare the price by weight.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:40.670
You could be paying 2 -300% to not have to wield the knife yourself.

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:45.380
Plus they often have a shorter shelf-life,
and you’re creating more packaging waste.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:48.702
That’s why I chop my own vegetables FIVE
days a week.

00:01:48.702 --> 00:01:55.040
Oh, and by the way, that mist that makes everything
seem fresh and crisp?

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.420
It’s pretty much just there for show.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.689
Constantly spraying water on produce can increase
the health risks of bacteria and make certain

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:05.020
fruits and veggies rot faster.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.349
It also makes the leafy greens weigh just
a bit more at checkout, so make sure to shake

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:10.700
‘em out before you bag ‘em.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:15.420
As you walk the aisles, remember to look up
and down, not just side-to-side.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.940
Stores put their most expensive items at eye-level,
hoping you won’t notice the cheaper alternatives

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:21.410
above and below.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.520
And yes, that applies to kid’s stuff, too.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.080
Excuse me, would you like to try a free sample?

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.660
Uh… sure, I guess.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.120
Free samples are more about your emotions
than your tastebuds.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.690
They create a feeling of personal obligation,
like you’re already in too deep to back

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:37.690
out.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:41.750
So if you’re gonna partake, be prepared
to make a clean break and move on.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.660
You’re just gonna walk away?

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.770
After everything we’ve been through?!

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.239
Supermarkets don’t just want you to buy
expensive stuff.

00:02:49.239 --> 00:02:52.210
They want you to buy more stuff in general.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:54.550
Take this so-called “bargain.”

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:58.569
Most people assume they have to purchase all
ten to get some perceived discount, but if

00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:03.710
you read the fine print, you’ll usually
find that the unit price is the same no matter

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:04.940
how many you buy.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:09.310
It’s also why shopping carts have been getting
bigger over the years.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:14.670
Marketing tests showed that shoppers spent
up to 40% more with a double-sized cart!

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:20.010
Those supersize carts also make it difficult
to do a U-turn in a narrow aisle so you’re

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.520
more likely to take the long way around.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:28.040
A longer path means more temptations, which
is why stores will periodically rearrange

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:32.300
the merchandise to keep even experienced shoppers
searching.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.690
Some people think that’s why essentials
like milk and eggs are always at the back

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:41.769
at the store, but store managers insist on
a more innocent reason: by law these items

00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:46.239
can’t be unrefrigerated for very long, and
since the delivery trucks unload at the back,

00:03:46.239 --> 00:03:48.519
it makes sense to put the fridges there.

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:52.959
I guess making shoppers walk through the whole
store is just a “happy accident.”

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:58.520
Once you’ve finally found everything you
need, there’s one more trial to overcome:

00:03:58.520 --> 00:03:59.690
the checkstand.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:05.220
This is where stores traditionally keep impulse
purchases like candy, magazines and gum because

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:12.550
they know that by now you’re experiencing
what psychologists call “decision fatigue.”

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:17.220
We get tired of being sensible and weighing
costs and benefits.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.530
It wears us down and makes us more open to
suggestion.

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:23.660
So it’s best to not even look at that stuff.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:28.060
“Don't look at it. Shut your eyes, Marion! Don't look at it, no matter what happens!"

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:32.620
These strategies have been refined over decades
to make shoppers spend more than they have

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.840
to and buy more than they need.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:38.850
Unfortunately, people who buy more tend to
consume more and waste more.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.440
It’s kind of a lose/lose/lose situation.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.460
But shopping for groceries and cooking at
home is still cheaper and healthier than eating

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:50.090
out all the time--which is a major reason
why Millennials are having trouble saving

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:51.090
money.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:55.460
So by all means: go to the supermarket…
just be prepared!

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:04.020
First off, and most importantly, make a list even if you have to write on a napkin in the parking lot. Don't go through those doors without it.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:09.870
The fewer choices you have to make in the
store means less temptation and less fatigue.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:14.410
If your store has them, use smaller carts
to discourage overbuying.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:17.460
Although those handbaskets may not be a good
idea.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:22.470
Studies found that the physical act of lifting
and carrying handbaskets made shoppers feel

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.340
like they deserved junk-food rewards.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.350
The brain’s a funny thing, isn’t it?

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:31.000
Bring headphones and listen to upbeat music.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:37.009
It will keep you fast and focused.

00:05:37.009 --> 00:05:39.670
And never go to the grocery store hungry.

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:43.500
That’s just asking your reptile brain to
take the driver’s seat.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:47.840
And if you still have trouble sticking to
the plan, leave your credit cards at home

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:50.979
and bring only what you want to spend in cash.

00:05:50.979 --> 00:05:55.310
Julia and I have been doing that for years
and it keeps temptation in check.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:59.930
Remember, these stores aren’t evil organizations
getting away with highway robbery.

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:05.430
In fact, as an industry, supermarkets have
one of the lowest profit margins in the U.S.,

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:08.910
so they rely on these mind-games just to stay
in the black.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:13.770
But you have a right to defend yourself, and
the best defense is understanding how your

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:15.259
own brain works.

00:06:15.259 --> 00:06:16.749
And that’s our two cents!

